<node id="Attention Mechanisms in Transformers (image)">
  <data key="d0">Attention Mechanisms in Transformers (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">The image illustrates the Scaled Dot-Product Attention and Multi-Head Attention mechanisms used in Transformer models. It highlights the modular and parallel processing capabilities, aligning with the detailed explanation of attention mechanisms in the surrounding text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-27baa1c784722bae36ce3229ffcade1a</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763841259</data>
</node>
<node id="Scaled Dot-Product Attention (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Scaled Dot-Product Attention (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation defines the scaled dot-product attention mechanism, which computes a weighted sum of values based on the compatibility of queries and keys, essential for the Transformer model's ability to process sequences effectively.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-89831d05bb14ee9d929c9d60e4a44635</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763841259</data>
</node>
<node id="Dot Product Variance in Attention Mechanism (discarded)">
  <data key="d0">Dot Product Variance in Attention Mechanism (discarded)</data>
  <data key="d1">discarded</data>
  <data key="d2">This content illustrates the variance of the dot product of query and key vectors in the attention mechanism, explaining why large values can occur and the need for scaling. It connects to the broader discussion on attention mechanisms in the Transformer model, emphasizing the importance of managing dot product magnitudes for effective training.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1365f2f56667b5c1a67a2f498774ef87</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763841259</data>
</node>
